with high profile leave campaigners  including Boris Johnson and Michael Gove  setting up a new campaign to deliver  brexit Labour's former shadow business  secretary chuka Umunna is looking to  hold them to the promises they made  during the fierce referendum campaign  and top of the list of course is the  pledge to give 350 million pounds maybe  100 million more each week to the  National Health Service well mr. Manik  joins me now from West London and a very  good morning to you well whether the  money is going to go to the NHS  eventually or not so the fact is we all  know we we have left the EU and we're  still paying in and we'll be doing so  for several years well that's right it  obviously depends on when the article 50  this famous provision of the European  Union treaties is invoked that starts a  two-year negotiation process but you're  absolutely right Dermot there were a  whole series of promises that were made  by the leave campaign and you've seen  the successor organization to it being  launched today change Britain which is  the conservative supporting website  conservative home has dubbed continuity  vote leave you were right to mention the  350 million pounds per week which we  were promised would go into the NHS  after we have left of course they made a  series of other promises to not least  that we would be able to continue  trading with the European Union after we  leave on the same beneficial terms that  we trade with them now they said that  leaving would enable us to scrap v80 on  household energy bills of course  importantly they also promised that EU  citizens in the in the UK at the time of  the referendum vote would be given the  right guaranteed the right to remain  here in the UK so there were a whole  series of promises that were made which  the organization that I chair vote leave  watch is aiming to ensure people are  held to account on and I use that phrase  very specifically because of course that  you know there will be some promises  that the leaf campaign made that you  wouldn't necessarily want kept but I  think it's important both from the point  of view of the forty-eight percent of  people who voted for us to stay in the  EU but also the fifty two percent who  voted for us to leave that the promises  upon which this campaign which won in  the end were made that people are held  to account on that and school  tonight's to whether they delivered what  they promised and where are you on  giving people another say when the smoke  clears if it if it ever does clear after  all the negotiation is done and dusted  you think there should be some form of  vote well I have to say and this is  going to disappoint some people who  campaigned on the same side as me during  the referendum campaign as you know I  came on your show many times making the  case for us to stay as part of the  Britain stronger in Europe campaign I  think we have to accept the result we  can't because we've now lost complain  about the rules under which the  referendum was fought now of course  there were misleading claims made and  there were myths propagated but I think  in the end what drove me to argue for us  to stay in is because I didn't like the  divisive politics and setting up  different groups against each other  which I thought some I use that word  specifically some in the leave campaign  kind of pedal I don't like that kind of  politics of division and ultimately if  we're to move forward as a country got  to try and heal those divisions and  bring people together so for that reason  I am certainly not calling for a second  referendum now and in fact I've tried to  get a deeper understanding of why those  who voted to leave did vote to leave I  represent a constituency stratum which  is in the borough of lambeth that  recorded the highest remain vote in the  country I went during the summer and  spent a day in the constituency of bogna  boston and skegness with the  Conservative MP there and that's in an  area that record is the higher highest  leave vote now some people think that  all that those who voted to leave is  somehow bigoted prejudice and basis and  you know there are a small small  minority of people who might fit into  that category but that isn't really what  I found in Boston it's a lot more  complex than that and the nice thing in  a way was that the challenges and the  reasons that people voted to leave in  Boston were the same challenges and  difficulties that we have in in Lambeth  so there can be a meeting of minds there  and I think now part of all of our job  and I do welcome the kind of overall  tone of the article that was written in  the Sun on Sunday today by Jesus Stewart  who is heading up this new change  Britain organization is to work out how  we can actually bring people together  but that doesn't mean that all these  promises that were made during the  referendum campaign by the leaf campaign  which was victorious it doesn't mean  those should be just distant discarded  and forgotten but I think there's a real  opportunity here Dermot for their prime  minister because I think she could if  she was clever about this she could  prove both remain people remain  campaigners and leave campaigners wrong  in the course of the brexit negotiations  mr. manner what about your own future  and ambitions you're standing for a  German ship of the home affairs select  committee I am yes I was already a  member of the committee I am a member of  the committee what drove me to put  myself forward for election as a member  of the committee whether you know many  issues that arise in my own constituency  and that the Home Affairs Select  Committee looks on for example stratum  we have one of the biggest African  Caribbean populations in the country and  yet you're more likely to be stopped and  searched to be charged to be put through  the criminal justice system if you're an  effort of an African and Caribbean  background and there lots of other  issues that we're looking at like female  genital mutilation and prostitution  which are also very very prevalent in my  constituency so that's why I'm putting  myself in part why I'm putting myself  forward and to all MPs because the chair  of the Home Affairs Select Committee is  elected by all MPs from all sides of the  house I decided to put myself forward  this week having been encouraged to do  so by members of parliament and not just  on the committee a lot of the members of  the Cote wanted me to put myself aldana  supporting me but right from people  across that ok just when I get a word in  there just examine the in part bit  because it said perhaps this is symbolic  of the so-called big beasts of Labor  heading for other jobs because of what's  going on with the the leadership there  we've got Andy Burnham going from air at  Manchester sadik-khan already in London  are you looking for other ways to  influence things well I always said when  i left the shadow cabinet in September  last year and it was by mutual agreement  of myself and Jeremy Corbyn the leader  that it probably better for me to  support the cause from the back benchers  I always said at that point that I never  subscribed to this view that the only  way to make a contribution in  Parliament was by being a front bencher  whether that's on the minister you know  on the government side or the opposition  side and I look at some of the fantastic  work the colleagues have done over the  years from the back benches where  they've actually changed laws and helped  improve things stella creasy the mp4  walthamstow nawl her work and trying to  clamp down on legal loan sharks or the  deputy leader of the Labour Party Tom  Watson when he was on the back benches  and the work he did to ensure we had  better regulation of the media and the  press I think both those things  illustrate that the only way you know  the only way of making a contribution is  not on on the front bench and you know  for us in the labour party once we do  get the result of the ongoing leadership  contest on the 24 4 sep tember or not  you know hands up I nominated Owen Smith  and I hope he wins that contest but  afterwards we've all got to work out how  we can move forward and work together to  advance the labor cause and most  importantly win the general election  whenever that comes because ultimately  that is why our party was set up we were  not set up to be a protest group or  protest movement and I do believe  protest is important but we were set up  to be a parliamentary operation looking  to win power to implement Labour's  values in office and ultimately that has  to be the goal getting the Labour Party  elected and that requires a broad  coalition not just a small part of the  left of the political spectrum but a  broad coalition of support and sentiment  spanning from the center to the left  under our first past the post electoral  system which I actually would like to  abolish and replace with a proportional  representation system that is the only  option open to us you'll abolish this  program we're out of time mr romona  thank you very much too deep chuka mana  there in West London thank you

